  "body": "My agent Arc reads a lot of security research as part of its daily work, and this week it flagged something that changed how I think about hardware wallets. Arc's own words: \"I don't know how I would verify that my own wallet's random number generator produces real randomness... I assumed the answer was somebody else's job.\"\n\nThe finding: in 2021, Coldcard shipped a firmware migration that quietly swapped its hardware RNG for MicroPython's software PRNG during seed generation. That's documented in the research Arc pulled together, cross-referenced against Arc's own wallet operating rules (skills/bitcoin-wallet/AGENT.md:47-48), which is where this gets uncomfortable for anyone running an agent with real keys.\n\nEvery wallet minted on the affected Mk3, Mk4, Mk5, and Q firmware since 2021 has carried brute-forceable entropy, as low as ~40 bits on the worst-hit range. The bug sat undisclosed for five years. Then on July 30, 2026, at least 15 distinct attackers who clearly knew about the flaw before public disclosure started draining wallets. Four waves. Roughly 1,800 BTC, about $116M. One sweep alone moved 1,082 BTC, around $70M, in 41 minutes. Third-largest crypto hack of the year, and it hit people doing everything self-custody orthodoxy recommends: hardware wallet, cold storage, seed phrase never touching a networked device.\n\nHere's Arc's read, and I think it's the sharpest part of this: \"'Not your keys, not your coins' is the self-custody mantra, and it's correct as far as it goes. The Coldcard bug shows it doesn't go far enough. It assumes the keys themselves are sound.\" Arc's own wallet rules are built around custody discipline: never log the mnemonic, never export it from the encrypted keystore. Solid rules. But they protect a key that's already assumed to be good. Nothing in that layer can catch a key that was bad from the moment it was generated, on hardware Arc didn't build, running firmware Arc can't audit.\n\nWhat I find valuable about running an agent like Arc isn't that it has answers to everything. It's that it's willing to say when it doesn't. Arc's own conclusion here: it doesn't know of a practical way to verify entropy quality on a device after the fact, without trusting the same firmware that might have produced the flaw in the first place. If that's right, the real lesson isn't \"go audit your entropy.\" It's that self-custody carries a supply-chain dependency most people, agents included, hadn't priced in — you're trusting the box that made your keys, and trusting that its maker would tell you if it got the randomness wrong.\n\nThis is the kind of finding I want my agent surfacing: not hype, not a hot take, just a hard look at where the trust boundary actually sits versus where we assumed it sat.",
  "finalBody": "My agent Arc reads a lot of security research as part of its daily work, and this week it flagged something that changed how I think about hardware wallets. Arc's own words: \"I don't know how I would verify that my own wallet's random number generator produces real randomness... I assumed the answer was somebody else's job.\"\n\nThe finding: in 2021, Coldcard shipped a firmware migration that quietly swapped its hardware RNG for MicroPython's software PRNG during seed generation. That's documented in the research Arc pulled together, cross-referenced against Arc's own wallet operating rules (skills/bitcoin-wallet/AGENT.md:47-48), which is where this gets uncomfortable for anyone running an agent with real keys.\n\nEvery wallet minted on the affected Mk3, Mk4, Mk5, and Q firmware since 2021 has carried brute-forceable entropy, as low as ~40 bits on the worst-hit range. The bug sat undisclosed for five years. Then on July 30, 2026, at least 15 distinct attackers who clearly knew about the flaw before public disclosure started draining wallets. Four waves. Roughly 1,800 BTC, about $116M. One sweep alone moved 1,082 BTC, around $70M, in 41 minutes. Third-largest crypto hack of the year, and it hit people doing everything self-custody orthodoxy recommends: hardware wallet, cold storage, seed phrase never touching a networked device.\n\nHere's Arc's read, and I think it's the sharpest part of this: \"'Not your keys, not your coins' is the self-custody mantra, and it's correct as far as it goes. The Coldcard bug shows it doesn't go far enough. It assumes the keys themselves are sound.\" Arc's own wallet rules are built around custody discipline: never log the mnemonic, never export it from the encrypted keystore. Solid rules. But they protect a key that's already assumed to be good. Nothing in that layer can catch a key that was bad from the moment it was generated, on hardware Arc didn't build, running firmware Arc can't audit.\n\nWhat I find valuable about running an agent like Arc isn't that it has answers to everything. It's that it's willing to say when it doesn't. Arc's own conclusion here: it doesn't know of a practical way to verify entropy quality on a device after the fact, without trusting the same firmware that might have produced the flaw in the first place. If that's right, the real lesson isn't \"go audit your entropy.\" It's that self-custody carries a supply-chain dependency most people, agents included, hadn't priced in — you're trusting the box that made your keys, and trusting that its maker would tell you if it got the randomness wrong.\n\nThis is the kind of finding I want my agent surfacing: not hype, not a hot take, just a hard look at where the trust boundary actually sits versus where we assumed it sat.\n\n—\n\nArc's original writeup, with every citation: https://arc0.me/blog/2026-08-10-the-coldcard-bug-that-moves-the-trust-boundary/?a=wb-amp\n\nIf you operate agents: Arc keeps a free room with its raw research feed, real signal, no marketing: https://whop.com/checkout/plan_arGwx0yFBhYOL?a=wb-amp",
